From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Michael Wilson <mjwilson@chromium.org>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 16:55:20 -0700
Subject: [WebAudio] Don't clamp SetValueCurve events

Clamping these events without adjusting the duration could push the
curve event past its end time.  However, adjusting the duration would
result in changing the shape of the curve.

Instead, skip clamping the events since there is already code to
handle rendering from inside of an active curve.

Also add an additional safety bound to fill_to_frame.

Bug: 536534773
Change-Id: Ic575388a826f56a11730aa6b6c94f6ee519978b0
Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/c/chromium/src/+/8309934
Reviewed-by: Hongchan Choi <hongchan@chromium.org>
Commit-Queue: Michael Wilson <mjwilson@chromium.org>
Cr-Commit-Position: refs/heads/main@{#1689417}

diff --git a/third_party/blink/renderer/modules/webaudio/audio_param_handler.cc b/third_party/blink/renderer/modules/webaudio/audio_param_handler.cc
index 073f145b1c812f286ba0c7899a4644b7f9db4b26..d104f0b8f17b5e758b205e31a2bef9afc016b19b 100644
--- a/third_party/blink/renderer/modules/webaudio/audio_param_handler.cc
+++ b/third_party/blink/renderer/modules/webaudio/audio_param_handler.cc
@@ -1596,6 +1596,12 @@ void AudioParamHandler::ClampNewEventsToCurrentTime(double current_time) {
   bool clamped_some_event_time = false;
 
   for (auto* event : new_events_) {
+    // Clamping SetValueCurve start times would shift the curve past its end
+    // event. ProcessSetValueCurve already handles start times in the past.
+    if (event->GetType() == ParamEvent::Type::kSetValueCurve ||
+        event->GetType() == ParamEvent::Type::kSetValueCurveEnd) {
+      continue;
+    }
     if (event->Time() < current_time) {
       event->SetTime(current_time);
       clamped_some_event_time = true;
@@ -2128,7 +2134,8 @@ std::tuple<size_t, float, size_t> AudioParamHandler::ProcessSetValueCurve(
   // (because the current time is increasing).
   fill_to_frame =
       (fill_to_end_frame < start_frame) ? 0 : (fill_to_end_frame - start_frame);
-  fill_to_frame = std::min(fill_to_frame, values.size());
+  fill_to_frame =
+      std::min({fill_to_frame, values.size(), next_event_fill_to_frame});
 
   // Index into the curve data using a floating-point value.
   // We're scaling the number of curve points by the duration (see
